    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s daily efficiency scores today are anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (79.96) and **Lawrence Butler** (74.61), both of whom present clear start-worthy profiles. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a highly favorable matchup against George Klassen (5.56 xERA), and despite the slight negative adjustment for the catcher's pop time, his volume of expected production in the three-hole makes him our premier asset. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** finds a lucrative situation against Zebby Matthews; the park factors, combined with a significant dynamic platoon advantage and a favorable spot in the order, drive his score well above the mid-tier baseline. **Zack Gelof** also provides a high-floor start (62.22), as the combination of a generous park and the propensity for stolen bases against a slow-delivering pitcher like Matthews makes him an essential component of today\u2019s strategy to attack our scarcest category.\n\nThe bench decision process was narrow today, with **Tyler Freeman** (43.76) narrowly missing the cut behind **Jung Hoo Lee** (46.57). While **Tyler Freeman** offers decent contact, his projected placement at the bottom of the order and the negative SP-skill adjustment versus a tough arm like Cade Cavalli relegated him to the pine. Conversely, the optimizer favored **Jung Hoo Lee** for his superior dynamic platoon advantage, which outweighed the risk of facing Chase Burns. We remain cautious regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board; should any of our starters like **Cal Raleigh** or **Jonathan Aranda** be shifted lower in the batting order, their efficiency scores will drop immediately, potentially forcing a re-evaluation against secondary bench options like **Gleyber Torres** or **Gavin Sheets**. Investors should monitor the early reports closely to ensure no unexpected scratch occurs, particularly given the aggressive approach required to maintain our competitiveness in steals and AVG.",
